# Rolograph — AI Rules

Built for Cursor Miami: Ship Night, August 6 2026. These rules governed how Cursor
was used to build this project and how it should be extended.

## Product intent

Rolograph turns an event into a living relationship graph. It answers four questions
that flat contact tools cannot: why does this person matter right now, how are they
connected to everyone else in the room, what was actually said, and what happens next.

The problem is relationship memory loss at events. Optimize every decision for that
problem, not for visual novelty.

## Scope discipline

The PRD was locked at 6:30 PM and lives in `PRD.md`. Under Ship Night rules, features
may be cut after the lock but never added.

- Do not add capabilities that are absent from `PRD.md`.
- Do not edit `PRD.md` itself. It is the locked promise judges score against.
- When time is short, deepen an existing PRD item rather than starting a new one.
- `PRD.md` lists an explicit out-of-scope section. Respect it: no auth, no database,
  no CRM integrations, no scraping, no identity resolution.

## Architecture

- Next.js App Router, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, GSAP, hand-rolled SVG graph.
- Client-side state only. There is no backend and no persistence layer by design.
- Graph nodes and edges are typed data in `app/page.tsx`. Keep them as plain typed
  arrays so the data model stays readable in a live demo.
- `app/globals.css` owns the entire visual system. Do not scatter one-off styles.
- No external graph layout library. Node positions are art-directed percentages
  because the demo needs choreography more than general-purpose layout.

## Data integrity

This is the rule that matters most.

- Never invent a person's title, employer, or relationship. Unverified identity is
  represented honestly — Amy Street is deliberately left as a host node marked
  `confidence: "Observed"` and tagged `needs enrichment` rather than assigned a
  plausible-sounding biography.
- Every node carries a `confidence` value. Distinguish observed edges from verified
  ones. Do not promote an edge to `High` without a real source.
- Event facts (schedule, venue, deadlines, hosts, sponsors) come from the official
  Luma listing and the Ship Night site. Do not paraphrase them into something wrong.

## Motion

- Motion must carry meaning. Animate to direct attention to a node, an edge, or a
  state change — never as decoration that competes with the signal.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`. The entrance timeline is skipped entirely when set.
- GSAP timelines must be killed on unmount and on presenter stop. Leaked timelines
  fighting each other is the most likely way this demo breaks on stage.
- Animate transforms and opacity. Avoid animating layout-triggering properties.

## Typography

An earlier ultra-bold all-caps treatment was rejected for looking like generic
hackathon filler and for clipping at narrow widths.

- Keep the two-voice lockup: restrained sans for `Rolo`, high-contrast italic serif
  for `graph`.
- Sentence case for supporting copy. Uppercase is reserved for small monospace labels.
- Stay aggressive through color, graph density, and motion — not through shouting type.

## Copy

- Write for a stranger who has never heard of this product. Anyone should understand
  what Rolograph does within twenty seconds of the page loading.
- Lead with the problem, then the product. Never lead with the author.
- No first-person founder voice and no personal names in product copy.

## Build and deploy

- `npm run build` runs `next build --webpack`. Turbopack hits a CSS compiler crash in
  this environment. Do not switch it back.
- Deploy target is Netlify (`netlify.toml`). The public URL must always match `main`.
- Verify the deployed site after every push. A stale deploy is indistinguishable from
  a broken product to anyone opening the public link.

## Definition of done

A change is done when `npm run build` passes, the deployed URL reflects it, and every
success criterion in `PRD.md` can still be performed by a stranger without guidance.
